        INSERT of the block of DxfLeader.Dimldrblk turned and scaled so, or, where it names none, AutoCAD's
        closed filled arrow as a SOLID a third as wide as long, its corners the one on the right of the leader,
        the tip, and the one on its left; then one LWPOLYLINE through its vertices on its plane (a LINE between
        each two, a leader whose vertices stand off the plane of its normal, which a polyline cannot hold), or
        for a splined one (DxfLeader.IsSplined) one SPLINE of degree 3 through them, starting along its first
        segment and ending along its last, its knots the lengths of the chords. Where it has an arrow, the line
        starts at the arrow's back, the arrow's length along its first segment. Each has the leader's own look,
        its colour, true colour, linetype and lineweight, whatever its dimension style or its overrides draw a
        leader with, as AutoCAD 2021 gives them. The leader from (0, 0, 0) through (10, 10, 0) to (20, 10, 0)
        with an arrow 2.5 long gives a SOLID of the corners (2.06, 1.47, 0), (0, 0, 0) and (1.47, 2.06, 0), and a
        polyline from (1.77, 1.77, 0) through (10, 10, 0) to (20, 10, 0). The hook AutoCAD runs on under the
        words of a leader that has one and holds its words above or below its line (DxfLeader.Dimtad not nought),
        to where they end, is AutoCAD's to work out from its fonts, and is among them where the drawing's
        DxfDatabase.FontSearchPath measures the MTEXT it points to (check F1b): the last vertex moved along the
        hook to the far end of the words, \"ABC\" of txt.shx 2.5 high held at (22, 10, 0), its hook ending at
        (21.375, 8.125, 0), giving a polyline ending at (28.667, 8.125, 0)."""
    def GetClosestPointTo(self, givenPoint: GeoPoint3, extend: bool) -> GeoPoint3:
