        towards the base point, the line of that one vertex, as AutoCAD 2021 lands it (check D38b): from (30, 40,
        0), a dogleg from there along -x; a line after it goes to that leader. Words held at their top centre or
        right are met from their edges where the fonts measure them (check D38b). Where the drawing's
        DxfDatabase.FontSearchPath measures the words (one line in an SHX or TrueType font), a point at their
        middle or right of it is met on their right, as AutoCAD 2021 lands it (check F1b): the dogleg against x,
        ending the gap past their ink, \"ABC\" of txt.shx 4 high at (100, 50, 0), 10.67 wide, met from (250, 0,
        0) by a dogleg from (120.67, 48, 0) to (112.67, 48, 0), and from (108, 0, 0) too, while (103, 0, 0) is
        met on the left; words whose ink reaches below their baseline are as high as their height and that depth
        together, \"Ag\" met 2.67 below its top at the middle of its top line. A leader of no landing yet
        (DxfMLeader.AddLeader) is passed over, as AutoCAD 2021 passes it. Words a leader meets on a side of
        attachment 8 are underlined (DxfMLeader.SetTextAttachmentType). Words mirrored across their plane, which
        run against its x axis (294 of 1), held at their right, are met as AutoCAD 2021 meets them where the
        fonts measure them (check D38): \"ABC\" of txt.shx held at (110.67, 50, 0) facing down, met from (250,
        100, 0) by a dogleg from (120.67, 48, 0). The line has no colour of its own (no 92), as AutoCAD 2021
        writes one."""
    @overload
    def AddLeaderLine(self, leaderIndex: int) -> int:
        """Adds a leader line of no vertices to a leader, as AutoCAD's AddLeaderLine of a leader: its index (91) one
        above every line's, of no colour of its own (no 92), drawn in DxfMLeader.LeaderLineColor, as AutoCAD 2021
        writes one (check D38). Its arrow goes at a vertex put in by DxfMLeader.AddFirstVertex."""
    def Explode(self) -> System.Collections.Generic.IReadOnlyList[DxfEntity]:
        """Gets the entities the multileader breaks into, as AutoCAD 2021's Explode gives them from its context
        data: its content first, an MTEXT of its words (DxfMLeader.MText, as wide as the context data says, which
        the scale has scaled already, as AutoCAD 2021 writes it, in its text style and colour) or an INSERT of
        its block (DxfMLeader.BlockContent at DxfMLeader.BlockPosition, scaled, turned and coloured so, its
        attributes given the values the multileader holds), either in the multileader's own linetype and
        lineweight, as AutoCAD 2021 gives them; then, leader by leader, its dogleg, a LINE from the last point of
        the leader along its direction, of no length where the dogleg has none, as AutoCAD 2021 draws it, where
        the multileader has one, its leader lines are not splined and the leader runs left or right of the words,
        a leader of no line giving its dogleg alone and one of no landing yet (DxfMLeader.AddLeader) nothing, as
        AutoCAD 2021 draws them; then line by line, unless the line is invisible, an LWPOLYLINE through the
        vertices to the last point of the leader, and the arrowhead at the first vertex. A splined line is one
        SPLINE fitted through the same points, leaving along its first segment and reaching along its dogleg, its
        knots the lengths of the chords, as AutoCAD's. Without a dogleg, a line on the left or right of the words
        runs on to the end the dogleg would have; so does every line of a multileader whose leader lines are
        splined (DxfMLeader.LeaderLineType), which AutoCAD 2021 draws with no dogleg: two lines splined from (0,
        0) and (0, 100) to a dogleg from (90, 48) to (98, 48) give two SPLINEs fitted from the backs of their
        arrows to (98, 48) and no LINE. The arrowhead is drawn where the first segment is more than twice as long
        as the arrow, as AutoCAD draws it: an INSERT of the line's or the multileader's arrow block, turned to
        point away from the line, by an angle from nought to a whole turn, and scaled to the arrow's size, or
        AutoCAD's closed filled arrow as a SOLID a third as wide as long, its corners the one on the right of the
        line, the tip and the one on its left; the line then starts at the back of the arrow. Each part is on the
        multileader's layer, and a colour of BYBLOCK, as the style Standard gives the words and the lines, is the
        multileader's own colour, as AutoCAD 2021 gives it; the arrowhead has the multileader's own linetype, and
        the SOLID its own lineweight, the INSERT the lineweight of its line, as AutoCAD 2021 gives them: a
        multileader of lineweight 0.30 and linetype Continuous of its own, its leader lines 0.50, gives a SOLID
        of 0.30 or an INSERT of 0.50, both Continuous. A line is drawn with the type, colour, linetype,
        lineweight and arrow size of its own where its flags (93) say it has them, BYBLOCK among them, and its
        colour where it has no flags and its colour is not BYBLOCK, as ezdxf writes one; else with the
        multileader's (DxfMLeader.GetLeaderLineType, DxfMLeader.GetLeaderLineColor,
        DxfMLeader.GetLeaderLineWeight): a line splined of its own is one SPLINE among the straight lines of the
        others, one invisible of its own gives nothing, and a line of arrows 2 of its own under arrows 4 starts 2
        from its tip; a size of its own is unscaled, as the multileader's own (42), and drawn times the scale of
        the context data, 4 at a scale of 2, where a line of none is drawn with the size of the context data
        (140). The words on the right of two leader lines with a dogleg of 8 give an MTEXT, the dogleg's LINE,
        and for each line an LWPOLYLINE and a SOLID. A multileader scaled to nothing gives nothing. The lines
        AutoCAD draws under or over the words, as long as the words are wide, are AutoCAD's to work out from its
        fonts, and are not among them. A copy in no file, made by DxfEntity.Clone, finds the blocks, the linetype
        and the text style its record names in the drawing it was copied from, as a dimension's copy finds its
        block, and gives what the original gives, the values of its attributes among it; where that drawing has
        erased a block it names since, it is refused, rather than drawn without it."""
    def ExplodeBroken(self) -> System.Collections.Generic.IReadOnlyList[DxfEntity]:
        """Gets the entities the multileader breaks into, as DxfMLeader.Explode gives them, but with each leader
        line cut at its breaks (DxfMLeader.GetBreaks), as AutoCAD draws a multileader that DIMBREAK has broken:
        AutoCAD 2021's Explode gives the lines whole. A line from (0, 0, 0) to (100, 0, 0) broken from 40 to 50
        gives two polylines, from the back of its arrow to 40 and from 50 to the end. A line splined, and the
        arrows, doglegs and words, are as DxfMLeader.Explode gives them."""
    def GetArrowSize(self, leaderLineIndex: int) -> float:
        """Gets the size of a leader line's arrow, as AutoCAD's GetArrowSize of a line: its own (group 40 of the
        line) where it overrides the multileader's (bit 16 of its 93), else the multileader's own size (its 42),
        as AutoCAD 2021 reads it; unscaled, the arrow drawn times the DxfMLeader.Scale. A multileader of arrows 4
        scaled by 2 by DxfEntity.TransformBy reads 4 here, its DxfMLeader.ArrowSize 8."""
    def GetBlockAttribute(self, attDefId: DxfAttributeDefinition) -> DxfAttributeReference:
        """Gets the value of an attribute of the block, as AutoCAD's GetBlockAttribute: an ATTRIB in no file of the
        tag of the attribute's definition and the value the MULTILEADER gives it (group 302 after the 330 of the
        definition's handle); the definition's own value where the MULTILEADER gives none."""
    def GetBreaks(self, leaderLineIndex: int) -> System.Collections.Generic.IReadOnlyList[DxfLeaderBreak]:
        """Gets the breaks of a leader line, as AutoCAD 2021's DIMBREAK keeps them in the line (90, 11 and 12 of its
        LEADER_LINE): for each the segment of the line it is in and where it starts and ends, in the order the
        record has them. A line no DIMBREAK has touched has none."""
    def GetDogleg(self, leaderIndex: int) -> GeoVector3:
        """Gets the direction of a leader's dogleg (groups 11, 21 and 31 of the leader), as AutoCAD's GetDogleg, in
        the world."""
    def GetDoglegLength(self, leaderIndex: int) -> float:
        """Gets how long a leader's dogleg is, as AutoCAD's GetDoglegLength: its length as drawn (group 40 of the
        leader, 1 where the leader gives none, as ezdxf reads one) over the DxfMLeader.Scale, as AutoCAD 2021
        gives it, 8 for a dogleg drawn 16 at a scale of 2; nought for a leader that meets the words at their top
        or bottom, which has none."""
    def GetFirstVertex(self, leaderLineIndex: int) -> GeoPoint3:
        """Gets the first vertex of a leader line, where its arrow is, as AutoCAD's GetFirstVertex."""
    def GetLastVertex(self, leaderLineIndex: int) -> GeoPoint3:
        """Gets the last vertex of a leader line, as AutoCAD's GetLastVertex: the point of its leader the dogleg
        starts at (10 of the leader); where the leader meets the words on their left or right and its dogleg is
        not drawn (no DxfMLeader.EnableDogleg, or its leader lines splined), the end the dogleg would have, which
        the line runs on to, as AutoCAD 2021 gives it: a line from (0, 0, 0) whose leader's dogleg of 8 starts at
        (90, 48, 0) ends at (98, 48, 0) once the dogleg is taken away."""
    def GetLeaderIndex(self, leaderLineIndex: int) -> int:
        """Gets the index of the leader a line is of, as AutoCAD's GetLeaderIndex."""
    def GetLeaderIndexes(self) -> System.Collections.Generic.IReadOnlyList[int]:
        """Gets the indices of the leaders (90 of each), in their order, as AutoCAD's GetLeaderIndexes."""
    def GetLeaderLineColor(self, leaderLineIndex: int) -> DxfColor:
        """Gets the colour of a leader line, as AutoCAD's GetLeaderLineColor: its own (group 92 of the line) where
        it overrides the multileader's (bit 2 of its 93), BYBLOCK where it does so with no 92, or where the line
        has no flags and its colour is not BYBLOCK, as ezdxf writes and DxfMLeader.Explode draws such a line;
        else BYBLOCK, as AutoCAD 2021 reads a line of no colour of its own whatever the
        DxfMLeader.LeaderLineColor it is drawn in (check D38b): a new line of a multileader of green lines reads
        BYBLOCK, and is drawn green."""
    def GetLeaderLineIndexes(self, leaderIndex: int) -> System.Collections.Generic.IReadOnlyList[int]:
        """Gets the indices of the lines of a leader (91 of each), in their order, as AutoCAD's
        GetLeaderLineIndexes."""
    def GetLeaderLineType(self, leaderLineIndex: int) -> DxfLeaderType:
        """Gets how a leader line is drawn, as AutoCAD's GetLeaderLineType: its own type (group 170 of the line)
        where it overrides the multileader's (bit 1 of its 93), else DxfMLeader.LeaderLineType."""
    def GetLeaderLineWeight(self, leaderLineIndex: int) -> DxfLineWeight:
        """Gets the lineweight of a leader line, as AutoCAD's GetLeaderLineWeight: its own (group 171 of the line)
        where it overrides the multileader's (bit 8 of its 93), else DxfMLeader.LeaderLineWeight."""
    def GetOverridedMLeaderStyle(self, mleaderStyle: DxfMLeaderStyle) -> None:
        """Gives a style made in code the values the multileader is drawn with, as AutoCAD's
        getOverridedMLeaderStyle, named here as the library names its members: those of its multileader style
        (DxfMLeader.MLeaderStyle), and in place of each it overrides (a bit of its 90) its own, as ezdxf's
        MLeaderStyleOverride reads them. A multileader of the style Standard, its words 2.5 high of its own,
        gives DxfMLeaderStyle.TextHeight 2.5 and the style's arrows, 4 long. An override is the value the
        multileader's group gives, the style's kept where the multileader has no group of it, but for the arrows:
        a multileader of AutoCAD's closed filled arrow gives a style of no arrow block. Each is read as the
        multileader writes it, as MLeaderStyleOverride reads it: the height of the words (bit 16) and the landing
        gap (bit 5) from the context data, scaled (its 41 and 145, as drawn, which DxfMLeader.TextHeight and
        DxfMLeader.LandingGap read over the scale); the size of the arrows (bit 9) from the multileader's own 42,
        unscaled, as the style holds it; the length of the doglegs (bit 7) from its own 41, unscaled too, as
        AutoCAD 2021 writes it. A multileader of scale 2 with words 8 high, arrows 4 and doglegs 8 of its own,
        drawn 16, gives a style of words 8 high, arrows 4 and doglegs 8, whose scale is the multileader's 2 where
        it overrides that too (bit 24)."""
    def GetPlane(self) -> GeoPlane3:
        """Gets the plane of the MULTILEADER, as AutoCAD's GetPlane: through the origin of its context's plane
        (110), of its DxfMLeader.Normal."""
    def GetTextAttachmentType(self, leaderDirection: DxfLeaderDirectionType) -> DxfTextAttachmentType:
        """Gets where the leaders of a side meet the words, as AutoCAD's GetTextAttachmentType: the left (group
        173), the right (95), the top (273) or the bottom (272), the last two from R2010."""
    def GetVertex(self, leaderLineIndex: int, index: int) -> GeoPoint3:
        """Gets a vertex of a leader line, as AutoCAD's GetVertex, in the world: the last the end of its leader's
        landing, as DxfMLeader.GetLastVertex gives it."""
    def HasContent(self) -> bool:
        """Gets whether the MULTILEADER holds the content its type says, as AutoCAD's HasContent: words or a block
        in its context data."""
    def MoveMLeader(self, vec: GeoVector3, type: DxfMoveType) -> None:
        """Moves the MULTILEADER or a part of it along a vector, as AutoCAD's MoveMLeader: everything, as
        DxfEntity.TransformBy by the move does; everything but the first vertex of each leader line, where its
        arrow is; or the content and the doglegs, the points they start at with them, the leader lines' own
        vertices staying."""
    def RemoveFirstVertex(self, leaderLineIndex: int) -> None:
        """Takes the first vertex of a leader line out, as AutoCAD's RemoveFirstVertex, down to one, the point its
        leader's dogleg starts at, as AutoCAD 2021 takes it: the line from (0, 0, 0) to the words \"ABC\" at
        (100, 50, 0) is left the one vertex (90, 48, 0), written as a line of no vertex of its own, and drawn as
        its dogleg alone, from (90, 48, 0) to (98, 48, 0), with no arrow. A line keeps that vertex, which AutoCAD
        2021 crashed taking out of a drawing it had read."""
    def RemoveLastVertex(self, leaderLineIndex: int) -> None:
        """Takes the last vertex of a leader line out, as AutoCAD's RemoveLastVertex, down to one: the vertex before
        it becomes the point the leader's dogleg starts at, as it stands, the content left where it is, as
        AutoCAD 2021 makes it. The line from (0, 0, 0) to the words \"ABC\" at (100, 50, 0) is landed at (0, 0,
        0), its dogleg drawn to (8, 0, 0); with no dogleg drawn (DxfMLeader.EnableDogleg) the dogleg starts at
        (0, 0, 0) all the same, and the vertex reads (8, 0, 0), the end the dogleg would have, as
        DxfMLeader.GetLastVertex reads it. A line keeps one vertex, as DxfMLeader.RemoveFirstVertex says; and the
        last vertex of a leader of more lines than one, the landing they all end at, is not taken out, as AutoCAD
        2021 refuses it."""
    def RemoveLeader(self, index: int) -> None:
        """Takes a leader out with its lines, as AutoCAD's RemoveLeader; the arrows of its lines (94 and 345) with
        them."""
    def RemoveLeaderLine(self, leaderLineIndex: int) -> None:
        """Takes a leader line out, as AutoCAD's RemoveLeaderLine; its arrow (94 and 345) with it, its leader
        kept."""
    def SetArrowSize(self, leaderLineIndex: int, arrowSize: float) -> None:
        """Gives a leader line's arrow a size of its own, as AutoCAD's SetArrowSize of a line: its group 40 written
        and bit 16 of its flags (93) set, from R2010; a line of a multileader of arrows 4 long given 2.5 reads
        2.5, the others 4. The size is unscaled, as DxfMLeader.ArrowSize is: DxfMLeader.Explode draws it times
        the DxfMLeader.Scale, a line given 4.5 on a multileader of scale 2 drawn 9. The size the arrow has
        already is written and flagged all the same, as AutoCAD 2021 writes it, from R2010."""
    def SetBlockAttribute(self, attDefId: DxfAttributeDefinition, attributeValue: DxfAttributeReference) -> None:
        """Gives an attribute of the block a value, as AutoCAD's SetBlockAttribute: the 302 of the definition's
        entry written, or an entry made after the others (its handle 330, index 177, width 44 of 1 and value
        302), as ezdxf writes one, from R2007."""
    def SetBreaks(self, leaderLineIndex: int, breaks: System.Collections.Generic.IEnumerable[DxfLeaderBreak]) -> None:
        """Sets the breaks of a leader line, in place of those it has, as AutoCAD 2021 writes them: grouped by
        segment, the segments in order, after the line's vertices. DxfMLeader.Explode gives the line whole, as
        AutoCAD's does; DxfMLeader.ExplodeBroken gives it cut at the breaks, as AutoCAD draws it. A line of one
        segment, from (0, 0, 0) to (100, 0, 0), given a break from (39.9375, 0, 0) to (40.0625, 0, 0) is drawn in
        two, to 39.9375 and from 40.0625."""
    def SetDogleg(self, leaderIndex: int, vector: GeoVector3) -> None:
        """Gives a leader's dogleg its direction, as AutoCAD's SetDogleg. A leader of no landing yet
        (DxfMLeader.AddLeader), or one whose dogleg is turned to another direction, is landed on the side of the
        content its dogleg now runs from, where the library can tell where it meets the content, as AutoCAD 2021
        lands it: along x on the left of the words, on either side of a block; on the right of the words, which
        AutoCAD lands by their font's width, it keeps its point, but where the drawing's
        DxfDatabase.FontSearchPath measures them, where it is landed as far right as they are wide. The box of
        lines from (-10, -5) to (20, 15) at (100, 50, 0) is met against x from (128, 55, 0), where a leader just
        added lands, and where its leader from the left lands once turned; a vertex then given a line of it goes
        before the landing."""
    def SetDoglegLength(self, leaderIndex: int, doglegLength: float) -> None:
        """Gives a leader's dogleg its length, as AutoCAD's SetDoglegLength: drawn times the DxfMLeader.Scale (group
        40 of the leader), as AutoCAD 2021 writes it, 2 for 1 at a scale of 2, and the bit of the overrides of
        the doglegs (7) set, the length it has too. The content or the leader moves as DxfMLeader.DoglegLength
        says: the content, where the leader is the only one of its side and none meets the content on the other;
        else the leader's start alone, as AutoCAD 2021 moves it: of two leaders landed at (150, 38, 0) on the
        left of words at (160, 40, 0), the second given 15 starts at (143, 38, 0), the words and the first
        staying."""
    def SetFirstVertex(self, leaderLineIndex: int, point: GeoPoint3) -> None:
        """Moves the first vertex of a leader line, as AutoCAD's SetFirstVertex, as DxfMLeader.SetVertex moves
        one."""
    def SetFromStyle(self) -> bool:
        """Gives the multileader the values of its multileader style and takes away its overrides of it, as
        AutoCAD's SetFromStyle: the style's scale first, then its look of the leader lines, the landing, the
        dogleg, the arrows, the words and the block, each written by the property of its name (DxfMLeader.Scale,
        DxfMLeader.LeaderLineType ... DxfMLeader.BlockConnectionType) in the multileader and its context data,
        its linetype (341) and text style (343, and 340 of its words) by the style's handles; then its overrides
        (90) set to nought. A multileader of the style Standard, its words 2.5 high of its own, has words 4 high,
        as the style. The sizes, unscaled in the style, are scaled once by the scale the multileader then has:
        the height of the words, the landing gap and the size of the arrows of the context data (41, 145 and 140)
        and each leader's length of the dogleg (40) are the style's times the scale, and the multileader's own
        size of the arrows (42) and length of the doglegs (41) the style's, unscaled, as AutoCAD 2021 writes
        them; Standard scaled 2 gives words 8 high, a gap of 4, arrows 8 drawn (42 of 4) and doglegs 16 drawn (41
        of 8). A value the style gives that the multileader cannot take keeps the multileader's own and its bit:
        a content (172) or a block (344) it does not hold, a size or a type out of AutoCAD's range, a linetype or
        a text style the style names none of or one its drawing has not, an arrow block or a block a copy in no
        drawing cannot be given (it keeps its own arrow, and takes the rest), and the attachments of the top and
        bottom and their direction in a file older than R2010; so do the bits of no property the library writes
        (18, 26 and 30). What it cannot take is judged before anything is written, so that nothing is left half
        set. The overrides of each leader line (its 93) are the multileader's own, not the style's, and stay. The
        words, the block and the landings stay where they are, the properties written here not moving them as
        they do when set one by one: how AutoCAD 2021 moves them when a style is taken has not been read."""
    def SetLastVertex(self, leaderLineIndex: int, point: GeoPoint3) -> None:
        """Moves the last vertex of a leader line, as AutoCAD's SetLastVertex: the point of its leader the dogleg
        starts at, or, where the dogleg is not drawn, that point so that the end the dogleg would have is there
        (DxfMLeader.GetLastVertex): (98, 48, 0) set to (50, 60, 0) puts the dogleg's start at (42, 60, 0), as
        AutoCAD 2021 puts it. The words or block and the other landings move with it, as DxfMLeader.SetVertex
        says; DxfMLeader.AddLastVertex moves neither, as AutoCAD's."""
    def SetLeaderLineColor(self, leaderLineIndex: int, leaderLineTypeId: DxfColor) -> None:
        """Gives a leader line a colour of its own, as AutoCAD's SetLeaderLineColor: its group 92 written and bit 2
        of its flags (93) set, from R2010; DxfMLeader.Explode draws the line in it. BYBLOCK sets the flag and
        writes no 92, as AutoCAD 2021 writes it (check D38b). The colour the line is drawn in already is written
        and flagged all the same, as AutoCAD 2021 flags it."""
    def SetLeaderLineType(self, leaderLineIndex: int, leaderLineType: DxfLeaderType) -> None:
        """Gives a leader line a type of its own, as AutoCAD's SetLeaderLineType: its group 170 written and bit 1 of
        its flags (93) set, as the DXF reference and the ODA's specification of DWG place them after its index
        (91), from R2010. The type the line is drawn with already is written and flagged all the same, as AutoCAD
        2021 flags it, from R2010."""
    def SetLeaderLineWeight(self, leaderLineIndex: int, leaderLineWeight: DxfLineWeight) -> None:
        """Gives a leader line a lineweight of its own, as AutoCAD's SetLeaderLineWeight: its group 171 written and
        bit 8 of its flags (93) set, from R2010. The lineweight the line is drawn with already is written and
        flagged all the same, as AutoCAD 2021 flags it."""
    def SetPlane(self, value: GeoPlane3) -> None:
        """Puts the multileader in a plane, as AutoCAD's SetPlane: the plane of its context data the one given, its
        origin (110) the plane's and its x axis (111) the one it had laid in the plane, the x axis of the plane's
        own system where it stood along the normal, its y axis (112) across them, or as it was where it lies in
        the plane already, on the side the x axis keeps, so that DxfMLeader.Normal is the plane's whichever way
        297 turns it; every point moved straight onto the plane (the base point of its content, the place of its
        words and block, the last point of each leader, the vertices of its lines and the points of their
        breaks); and every direction laid in it (the words' direction and turn, the block's turn and matrix, the
        doglegs), the normals of its words and block the plane's, or the other way where they faced away from its
        plane before, as the words of a mirrored multileader (297 of 1, its words of normal -z over a plane of
        +z) are read from behind it. The multileader of the floor whose words are at (800, 0, 0) put in the plane
        z = 5 has its words at (800, 0, 5) and its leaders' vertices 5 up; put in a plane x = 0 its words go to
        (0, 0, 0). A point and a direction in the plane already keep their numbers, of whatever length, and axes
        that lean to each other, as a shear leaves them, keep theirs, so that the multileader put in its own
        plane changes nothing."""
    def SetTextAttachmentType(
        self,
        textAttachmentType: DxfTextAttachmentType,
        leaderDirection: DxfLeaderDirectionType,
    ) -> None:
        """Sets where the leaders of a side meet the words, as AutoCAD's SetTextAttachmentType: the MULTILEADER's
        group of the side and that of its context data, and the side's bit of the overrides. Where leaders meet
        the words on that side, the words or the landings move as AutoCAD 2021 moves them, the words running
        along the plane: the left set, the words move up by as much more as the left is met below their top, the
        leaders on the right with them; the right set, the words move so where no leader meets them on the left,
        else the leaders on the right are landed lower by as much. Three lines at (100, 50, 0) met on both sides
        at the middle of their top line, the left set to the bottom of the bottom line, stand at (100, 65.33, 0),
        the dogleg on their right from (120.67, 63.33, 0). Attachment 8, the underline of every line, underlines
        the words where a leader meets them on its side, as AutoCAD writes it: \"ABC\" becomes \\LABC, words that
        begin with \\L or have no letters left; set away from it, \\L is taken off the words again."""
    def SetVertex(self, leaderLineIndex: int, index: int, point: GeoPoint3) -> None:
        """Moves a vertex of a leader line, as AutoCAD's SetVertex: one of its own; the last, the end of its
        leader's landing (DxfMLeader.GetLastVertex), which moves the end of every line of the leader with it,
        and, for a leader on a side of the content, the words or block and the landings of the other leaders on a
        side by as much, as AutoCAD 2021 moves them: words \"ABC\" at (100, 50, 0) met from (90, 48, 0), the last
        vertex set to (50, 60, 0), stand at (60, 62, 0)."""
    def VerticesCount(self, leaderLineIndex: int) -> int:
        """Gets how many vertices a leader line has, as AutoCAD's VerticesCount: its own (10 of the line), then the
        end of its leader's landing (DxfMLeader.GetLastVertex), which every line of the leader ends at; its own
        alone where the leader has no landing yet (DxfMLeader.AddLeader), none for a line just added to it, as
        AutoCAD 2021 counts them."""

class DxfMLeaderStyle(DxfDBObject):
    """A multileader style of a DXF file, as AutoCAD's MLeaderStyle: an MLEADERSTYLE of the dictionary
    ACAD_MLEADERSTYLE (DxfDatabase.MLeaderStyleDictionary), the look a DxfMLeader of the style takes where it
    overrides none of its own."""
